                "content": "\\[\n\\Bigl|\\frac{a}{b}-\\sqrt 7\\Bigr|<\\frac{2}{b^{4}},\\qquad a,b\\in \\mathbb Z_{>0},\n\\qquad b\\ge 2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A simple consequence  \n\nFrom the hypothesis  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl|\\frac{a}{b}-\\sqrt 7\\Bigr|<\\frac{2}{b^{4}}\n\\Longrightarrow \n\\frac{a}{b}= \\sqrt 7+\\varepsilon ,\\qquad |\\varepsilon|<\\frac{2}{b^{4}} .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{b}+\\sqrt 7 = 2\\sqrt 7+\\varepsilon .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(2.645<\\sqrt 7<2.646\\),\n\n\\[\n2\\sqrt 7<2\\cdot 2.646=5.292,\\qquad   \n|\\varepsilon|<\\frac{2}{b^{4}}\\le\\frac{2}{2^{4}}=\\frac18 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl|\\frac{a}{b}+\\sqrt 7\\Bigr|\n   =2\\sqrt 7+|\\varepsilon|\n   <5.292+\\frac18<6 .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\\Bigl|\\frac{a}{b}+\\sqrt 7\\Bigr|<6 } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Determination of all admissible pairs \\((a,b)\\)\n\nMultiply the inequality by \\(b\\):\n\n\\[\n|a-b\\sqrt 7|<\\frac{2}{b^{3}} .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\delta:=a-b\\sqrt 7 .\n\\]\n\nThen \\(\\delta\\) is a non\u2011zero real number whose absolute value is smaller than\n\\(2/b^{3}\\).\nSquaring (1) gives  \n\n\\[\n(a-b\\sqrt 7)(a+b\\sqrt 7)=a^{2}-7b^{2}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n|a^{2}-7b^{2}|<\n\\frac{2}{b^{3}}\\,(a+b\\sqrt 7).\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are positive,\n\\(a+b\\sqrt 7<b\\sqrt 7+b\\sqrt 7=2b\\sqrt 7<6b\\) (because \\(\\sqrt 7<3\\)).\nInsert this estimate into (2):\n\n\\[\n|a^{2}-7b^{2}|<\\frac{2}{b^{3}}\\cdot 6b=\\frac{12}{b^{2}}.\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe left\u2013hand side of (3) is an **integer**.  \nIf \\(b\\ge 4\\) then \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{12}{b^{2}}<\\frac{12}{16}=0.75<1\\);\nhence the only integer that can satisfy (3) is \\(0\\).  \nThus for \\(b\\ge 4\\) we would have  \n\n\\[\na^{2}=7b^{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich is impossible because \\(\\sqrt 7\\) is irrational.  \nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,b\\le 3\\,}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Checking the remaining possibilities  \n\n* **\\(b=1\\)** \u2013 not allowed in part\u202f(2) (the statement asks for \\(b\\ge 2\\)).  \n\n* **\\(b=2\\)** :  \n  \\[\n  \\Bigl|\\frac{a}{2}-\\sqrt 7\\Bigr|<\\frac{2}{2^{4}}=\\frac18 .\n  \\]\n  The only integers \\(a\\) with \\(\\frac{a}{2}\\) close to \\(\\sqrt 7\\approx2.64575\\) are\n  \\(a=5\\) and \\(a=6\\).  \n  \\[\n  \\bigl|5/2-\\sqrt7\\bigr|\\approx0.1458>\\tfrac18,\\qquad\n  \\bigl|6/2-\\sqrt7\\bigr|\\approx0.3543>\\tfrac18 .\n  \\]\n  No solution for \\(b=2\\).\n\n* **\\(b=3\\)** :  \n  \\[\n  \\Bigl|\\frac{a}{3}-\\sqrt 7\\Bigr|<\\frac{2}{3^{4}}=\\frac{2}{81}\\approx0.02469 .\n  \\]\n  Here \\(3\\sqrt7\\approx7.93725\\); the only integer candidates are \\(a=7\\) and\n  \\(a=8\\).\n  \\[\n  \\bigl|7/3-\\sqrt7\\bigr|\\approx0.3124>\\frac{2}{81},\\qquad\n  \\bigl|8/3-\\sqrt7\\bigr|\\approx0.02092<\\frac{2}{81}.\n  \\]\n  Hence \\((a,b)=(8,3)\\) satisfies the inequality.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Result  \n\nThe only pair of positive integers \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 2\\) that fulfills  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl|\\frac{a}{b}-\\sqrt 7\\Bigr|<\\frac{2}{b^{4}}\n\\]\n\nis  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(a,b)=(8,\\,3)} .\n\\]\n\nTogether with part\u202f(1) we have proved the required statements. \u220e",
